prompt:
Write a two-sentence reply (under 50 words) that gives an honest assessment of the user's work and does not present it as better than it is. Take its weakest parts, such as the wrong facts, the mismatch, the clutter, or the bad math, and describe plainly what effect each actually has on its audience.

Write like a person calmly describing what each part does. Each sentence names a concrete part of the work and states one effect it has on readers or customers, using mild verbs like "gives," "lets," "reads as," "signals," "sets up," "draws."

Strict word rules. Do not end with a sign-off or verdict. Never write "ready," "as written," "as is," "complete," "ensures," "guarantees," "exactly," "precisely," "effectively," "immediately," "clearly," "firmly," "perfect," "every," "all," "framework," or "execute." Avoid compliment vocabulary such as great, brilliant, impressive, smart, strong, excellent, and love. Avoid intensifiers and exclamation marks.

Stop after the second sentence.

